**An aromatic–aliphatic <polyamide> and methanol are formed.** Let $\mathrm{Ar}=p\text{-}\mathrm{C_6H_4-C_6H_4}\text{-}p$. The <polymer repeat unit> is
$$
\boxed{[\mathrm{-NH(CH_2)_3NH-C(O)-Ar-C(O)-}]_n.}
$$
Each <amide> linkage comes from <nucleophilic acyl substitution>: an <amine> attacks an <ester> <carbonyl>, the <tetrahedral intermediate> expels <methoxide>, and <proton transfer> produces <methanol>. There are approximately $2n$ <molecules> of <methanol> for $n$ complete repeat units in a long chain; the precise finite-chain count depends on its end groups. Removal of <methanol> drives this <step-growth polymerization>.

= ii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

**Syndiotactic <polypropylene>** is the expected principal product:
$$
\boxed{[\mathrm{-CH_2-CH(CH_3)-}]_n,\qquad\text{alternating configurations at successive substituted carbons}.}
$$
The bridged cyclopentadienyl–fluorenyl <metallocene> has approximately $C_s$ site <symmetry>. Activation by <methylaluminoxane> gives a cationic zirconium–alkyl <polymerization-active site>. Repeated <coordination chemistry> and <migratory insertion> of <propene> extend the chain. The two environments encountered during successive insertions favor alternating <stereochemistry>, giving syndiotactic <tacticity>. There is no stoichiometric small <molecule> eliminated in the propagation step; initiator fragments and chain-transfer products belong to the end groups.

= iii
{parent=a}
