= Solution
{parent=iii}

**Predominantly head-to-tail poly(3-hexylthiophene)** is formed, with thiophene rings connected through their 2 and 5 positions. The hexyl groups occupy equivalent 3 positions along the <conjugated polymer>.

Selective iodine–magnesium exchange first generates an organomagnesium <monomer> while retaining the carbon–bromine bond. Nickel-catalyzed <Kumada coupling> then joins the organomagnesium end of one ring to the brominated end of another. Transfer of the nickel center to the new chain end permits <catalyst-transfer polymerization> and favors head-to-tail <regioregularity>. Organic side products of the activation include isopropyl iodide; magnesium halides accompany the coupling. The repeat unit itself contains neither iodine nor bromine.

= iv
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iv}

**An approximately alternating styrene–maleic anhydride copolymer** is produced. Its backbone alternates a $\mathrm{-CH_2-CH(Ph)-}$ segment with the two former alkene carbons of a maleic anhydride unit; those latter carbons remain connected to the intact five-membered <cyclic anhydride> ring.

<Homolysis> of <benzoyl peroxide> supplies initiating <free radicals>. Cross-propagation between electron-rich <styrene> and electron-poor <maleic anhydride> is favored over successive additions of the same <monomer>. Both carbon–carbon <double bonds> become backbone <sigma bonds>, without loss of a small <molecule> from each <monomer>. Initiator-derived benzoyloxy or phenyl fragments can occur at chain ends, with carbon dioxide formed when benzoyloxy <free radicals> decarboxylate.

= b
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

In (i), any pair of compatible functional groups can react: <monomers>, oligomers and long chains all participate. For an equimolar, strictly bifunctional mixture without <cyclization in step-growth polymerization>, the <Carothers equation> gives
$$
\boxed{\overline X_n=\frac{1}{1-p}.}
$$
Here $p$ is functional-group conversion and $\overline X_n$ counts the original <monomer> <molecules>. Large <degree of polymerization> therefore requires nearly complete conversion.

In (ii), growth occurs only at the metal-bound chain end. If initiation is rapid and <chain transfer in chain polymerization> is negligible, the number of chains is fixed and
$$
\overline X_n\simeq \frac{[M]_0}{[I]_0}p.
$$
This is the linear conversion dependence of <living polymerization>. Actual zirconocene systems can undergo chain transfer, so linear growth is a limiting model, not a consequence of <coordination polymerization> alone.

In (iv), a newly initiated chain rapidly attains a large size and then undergoes <radical termination>. Further conversion mostly creates additional chains. Thus high <degree of polymerization> can occur at low conversion, without the systematic linear increase of an ideal living system. <temperature>, initiator depletion and <monomer> depletion can change that size during the run.

\Image[paper-1-polymer-growth.png]
{title=Contrasting idealized degrees of polymerization during three growth mechanisms}

= c
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

For the ideal equimolar <step-growth polymerization>, $p=0.5$ gives
$$
\boxed{\overline X_n=2.}
$$
This counts individual starting <molecules>, so it is only roughly one diamine–diester pair per average chain, rather than a long <polymer>. In a successful <radical polymerization>, many <monomers> normally add between initiation and <radical termination>. **The styrene–maleic anhydride copolymer in (iv) therefore has the larger expected <degree of polymerization>.**

Conversion alone does not specify the radical-chain length: the conclusion assumes ordinary polymer-forming conditions, rather than arbitrarily high initiator concentration or extremely strong <chain transfer in chain polymerization>.

= d
{parent=1}
