= Solution
{parent=i}

The four shortest contacts are the three Cu–N bonds and Cu–OR bond. They define an approximately square equatorial set. The two remaining Cu–O contacts are longer and define a strongly elongated axial direction, as expected for the <Jahn–Teller effect> of $d^9$ Cu(II).

Choose $z$ along the long contacts and $x,y$ towards the short equatorial donors. The singly occupied, predominantly metal <magnetic orbital> is then **$d_{x^2-y^2}$**, with substantial <Ligand in coordination chemistry> admixture. All three pyrazine nitrogen donors lie in this equatorial set, so both ladder directions have strong metal–<Ligand in coordination chemistry> <orbital overlap>. Neighboring copper centers communicate through comparable pyrazine <superexchange> pathways. Virtual <electron> transfer between half-filled orbitals stabilizes the antiparallel arrangement, giving **antiferromagnetic interactions in both directions with comparable magnitudes**. The axial oxygen donors are much less effective exchange routes.

= ii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

At high <temperature>, $\chi T$ tends towards the nearly constant <Curie constant> of weakly correlated $S=\tfrac12$ centers. On cooling, $\chi T$ falls because neighboring <spins> increasingly correlate antiparallel. The broad maximum in $\chi$ marks the crossover from the high-temperature Curie-like regime to substantial <short-range order>.

At still lower <temperature>, the <Magnetic susceptibility> becomes small because the antiferromagnetic ladder favors a singlet-like ground state and has few thermally accessible magnetic excitations. An even-leg <spin ladder> can have a <spin gap>, which reinforces this suppression. **A broad <Magnetic susceptibility> maximum and falling $\chi T$ support short-range antiferromagnetic correlations; they do not establish a three-dimensional Néel transition.** Such a transition would require independent evidence, rather than identifying the broad maximum itself as the ordering <temperature>.

