= Solution
{parent=i}

For the same geometry, separation and intervening medium, the attractive <van der Waals force> scales with the effective <Hamaker constant>. With the constants treated as applying to those same conditions,
$$
\boxed{\frac{|F_{\rm Ag}|}{|F_{\rm PS}|}=\frac{40}{6.6}\simeq6.06.}
$$
**The silver particles attract more strongly.** This comparison is conditional on a common geometry and on using the appropriate constants across the actual medium. Constants for particles in vacuum cannot automatically be applied unchanged across a solvent.

= ii
{parent=d}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

For two equal spheres of radius $R$, the effective radius in the <Derjaguin approximation> is $R_{\rm eff}=R/2$. The nonretarded attractive <van der Waals potential> is
$$
U(h)=-\frac{A R_{\rm eff}}{6h}=-\frac{AR}{12h}.
$$
Differentiating with respect to the surface gap gives
$$
\boxed{F(h)=-\frac{AR}{12h^2}
=-\frac{(4.0\times10^{-19})(10^{-9})}{12(2\times10^{-9})^2}
=-8.3\times10^{-12}\ \mathrm N.}
$$
Thus the requested approximation gives **an attraction of about $8.3$ pN**. Here $h/R=2$, whereas the <Derjaguin approximation> requires $h\ll R$. This is the formal estimate requested by the hint, not a quantitatively reliable near-contact approximation for the printed dimensions.

= iii
{parent=d}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

The <Stern layer> is the compact near-surface layer of specifically adsorbed or closely associated ions. The <diffuse layer> is the more extended, thermally distributed excess of counterions that screens the surface charge.

For water near $25^\circ\mathrm C$, the <Debye length> can be written
$$
\lambda_D\simeq\frac{0.304\ \mathrm{nm}}{\sqrt{I/(\mathrm{mol\,L^{-1}})}},\qquad
I=\frac12\sum_i c_i z_i^2.
$$
For $0.01$ M KCl, $I=0.01$ M and **$\lambda_D\simeq3.0$ nm**. For a fully dissociated 2:2 <electrolyte> at the same formula-unit concentration, $I=4c=0.04$ M, giving **$\lambda_D\simeq1.5$ nm**, half as large. These are linear-screening estimates, not sharp boundaries of the <diffuse layer>.

= 5
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}

= a
{parent=5}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

In the <Asakura–Oosawa model>, nonadsorbing <polymers> are treated as ideal, mutually penetrable particles, but their centers cannot enter a layer around a hard <colloid>. The <polymer> <osmotic pressure> is $\Pi=\rho k_{\rm B}T$ in the ideal dilute limit, with the appropriate number density in the space dimension considered.

For fixed <polymer> number, reducing the inaccessible volume increases the available volume and lowers the <polymer> <Helmholtz free energy>. Equivalently, at fixed <polymer> <chemical potential>, the <polymer> contribution to the effective colloid potential is
$$
\boxed{U_{\rm dep}=\Pi\,\Delta V_{\rm excluded}=-\Pi V_{\rm overlap}.}
$$
When the excluded layers overlap, their union occupies less space than two separate layers. More <polymer> configurations become available, so the resulting <depletion force> attracts the colloids. It is an <entropy>-driven effective interaction, even though each microscopic colloid–<polymer> interaction is purely repulsive.

= b
{parent=5}
{scope}

= i
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

The unrotated squares have a gap $d>a$, so their facing exclusion layers do not overlap. Rotation can bring them into contact even though their centers remain fixed. The strongest <depletion force> comes from **parallel faces in contact over a long shared length**, rather than a corner-to-corner contact.

Let $r=\ell+d$. Rotate both squares through the same angle $\theta$ relative to their original orientation. Their center displacement has a face-normal component $r\cos\theta$ and a tangential component $r\sin\theta$. Face contact requires
$$
\boxed{\cos\theta_* =\frac\ell r,\qquad
\theta_*=\pm\arccos\frac\ell{\ell+d}\simeq\pm\sqrt{\frac{2d}{\ell}}.}
$$
The contacting faces overlap for length
$$
L=\ell-r|\sin\theta_*|=\ell-\sqrt{r^2-\ell^2}\simeq\ell-\sqrt{2\ell d}.
$$
These two mirror-related orientations, together with equivalent square rotations, maximize the leading overlap area when $a<d\ll\ell$. A small <polymer> gap sacrifices overlap width without a compensating leading increase in contact length.

= ii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Each face excludes <polymer> centers out to distance $a/2$. At face contact, the combined overlap strip has width $a$ and length $L$. Ignoring end corrections of order $a^2$, the two-dimensional <Asakura–Oosawa model> therefore gives
$$
\boxed{U_{\rm min}\simeq-\rho k_{\rm B}T\,a
\left[\ell-\sqrt{(\ell+d)^2-\ell^2}\right]
\simeq-\rho k_{\rm B}T\,a\ell.}
$$
The final expression retains only the leading term in $d/\ell$. Its dimensions are correct because $\rho$ is a number per area. The zero of this effective <potential energy> is the well-separated, nonoverlapping-exclusion-layer configuration.

= c
{parent=5}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

**There is no universal ranking from the information supplied.** The <depletion force> scales with <polymer> <osmotic pressure>, hence with $\rho$ in the ideal model. <London dispersion forces> instead depend on the material-dependent <Hamaker constant>, the intervening solvent, the closest approach and the contact geometry.

Close face contact can give a strong dispersion contribution; the ideal continuum expression even becomes singular without a molecular-distance cutoff. Nevertheless, refractive-index matching can greatly reduce that contribution, and increasing <polymer> concentration can strengthen <depletion force>. A quantitative comparison requires those missing material and concentration scales. The two attractive contributions may act together.

