= 10
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}

= a
{parent=10}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

A coherent mechanism is an **allene cyclization–<norbornene> insertion–cyclopropane closure cascade**. Label the propargyl chain $\mathrm{N-CH_2-C\equiv CH}$ as $D-E-F$. Base-assisted propargyl-to-allenyl isomerization gives $\mathrm{N-CH=C=CH_2}$. The hydrogen relocation is important: the terminal carbon must become a methylene before the final C–H substitution.

Reduction of the palladium precursor gives a phosphine-ligated Pd(0) <catalyst>. <Oxidative addition> into the aryl C–I bond supplies an aryl–Pd(II) intermediate. Intramolecular <carbopalladation> of the allene forms the aryl–$E$ bond and the six-membered lactam ring, retaining $D=E$ and placing Pd on the terminal carbon $F$. This allylic organopalladium connectivity may be represented by its interconverting sigma and pi forms.

<Norbornene> approaches from its less hindered exo face and inserts into the $F$–Pd bond. Carbon $F$ bonds to one former <norbornene> alkene carbon, while Pd bonds to the adjacent one. The resulting intermediate is geometrically unable to undergo an ordinary favorable <beta-hydride elimination>: the relevant syn arrangement is blocked, or would produce a bridgehead alkene.

Base-assisted activation of an $F$–H bond gives a cyclopropane-forming dialkylpalladium connectivity, represented as a four-membered Pd–C–C–$F$ ring. <Reductive elimination> closes the bond between $F$ and the Pd-bearing <norbornene> carbon, releases Pd(0), and gives the cyclopropane. The lactam alkene $D=E$ remains intact. Carbonate removes the acid equivalent generated in the overall arylation.

This mapping accounts for **the six-membered heterocycle, the new three-membered ring and exo addition to <norbornene>**. The <catalyst> and substrates are achiral, so the drawn relative <stereochemistry> represents a racemic product. This is a proposed organopalladium pathway; the question's product alone does not uniquely establish the lifetime or order of every palladium intermediate.

= b
{parent=10}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Disconnect the two new bonds of the <isoxazolidine> as a **<nitrone> \[3+2\] <cycloaddition>**. Suitable achiral partners are $\mathrm{PhCH=N^+(Ph)O^-}$ and the trans enone $\mathrm{(2\!\! -\!pyridyl\ N\!\! -\!oxide)CO-CH=CHPh}$.

A practical <catalyst> family is **Cu(II) with an enantiopure bis(oxazoline) <Ligand in coordination chemistry>**, for example a Cu(OTf)$_2$–BOX complex. The enone can bind through its <ketone> oxygen and pyridine N-oxide oxygen, giving an O,O-chelated assembly. Chelation both activates the conjugated double bond and fixes its orientation in the chiral pocket. Highly enantioselective <cycloadditions> of this substrate class with Cu(II)–BOX complexes are experimentally established.<Highly enantioselective nitrone cycloadditions with 2-alkenoyl pyridine N-oxides catalyzed by Cu(II)-BOX complexes by Santiago Barroso and others>[Abstract, Organic Letters 13, pages 436–439]{ref}

The <nitrone> carbon bonds to the enone $\alpha$ carbon and the <nitrone> oxygen bonds to the $\beta$ carbon. Thus the carbonyl-bearing carbon lies between the two phenyl-bearing ring carbons, as in the target. The trans enone gives the required trans relationship of its original acyl and phenyl substituents. Selection between endo/exo dipole approaches gives the remaining relative <stereochemistry>; the sterically preferred chelated transition-state arrangement places the two ring phenyl substituents on the same face and the acyl group on the opposite face, matching the drawing.

One <enantiomer> of the BOX <Ligand in coordination chemistry> exposes one enone face; its mirror image reverses the product's absolute <stereochemistry>. The exact <Ligand in coordination chemistry> substituents, metal counterion and <temperature> require optimization. The proposal identifies an experimentally supported <catalyst> platform and its stereochemical control elements, rather than claiming that an arbitrary BOX complex must give a specified numerical enantiomeric excess.

= 11
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}

= a
{parent=11}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

Take the dipoles to point along $+z$, and let their surface number density be $n_s$. An annulus of radius $\rho$ and width $d\rho$ contains $n_s2\pi\rho\,d\rho$ dipoles. The <electron>'s electrostatic <potential energy> is therefore
$$
U(z)=-\frac{en_sp}{4\pi\epsilon_0}\int_0^\infty
\frac{z\,2\pi\rho\,d\rho}{(\rho^2+z^2)^{3/2}}.
$$
For $z\ne0$, the integral without $2\pi$ equals $z/|z|$, giving
$$
U(z)=-\frac{en_sp}{2\epsilon_0}\operatorname{sgn}z.
$$
Thus the jump's magnitude is
$$
\boxed{|U(0^+)-U(0^-)|=\frac{en_sp}{\epsilon_0}
=\frac{e(p/A)}{\epsilon_0}.}
$$
For the chosen outward dipole orientation the electron-energy change from the negative to positive side is negative. The electrostatic voltage jump has the opposite sign. This distinguishes the requested magnitude from the sign of the <work function> change.

= b
{parent=11}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Let $\Gamma$ be coverage in $\mathrm{mol\,m^{-2}}$. Then $n_s=N_A\Gamma$ and the Helmholtz surface-dipole relation is
$$
\Delta\Phi_{\rm eV}=-\frac{N_A\Gamma p_\perp}{\epsilon_0},
$$
where a voltage in volts has the same numerical value as the electron-energy change in <electronvolts>. Converting the slope from square centimeters to square meters gives $-5\times10^5\ \mathrm{V\,m^2\,mol^{-1}}$. Therefore
$$
\boxed{p_\perp=\frac{\epsilon_0(5\times10^5)}{N_A}
=7.35\times10^{-30}\ \mathrm{C\,m}=2.20\ \mathrm D.}
$$
**Each added <molecule> produces an effective outward surface dipole of about $2.2$ D**, lowering the <electron> <work function>. This is the incremental adsorbate-plus-surface dipole, not necessarily the isolated molecular <electric dipole moment>.

= c
{parent=11}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

Nitrogen-down <adsorption> places the positive hydrogen end farther from the metal, producing an outward molecular <electric dipole moment> and the observed decrease in <work function>. Its maximum normal component, however, is only $1.42$ D. Tilting alone can only reduce that component; it cannot explain an effective $2.20$ D.

The adsorbed <molecule> and metal therefore undergo appreciable <electron density>. Lone-pair donation into the metal, polarization of the substrate, image response and distortion of the adsorbate can change the effective interface dipole. The inferred increase relative to an upright isolated <molecule> is about **$0.78$ D**. For scale, transferring about $0.16$ elementary charges over $1$ Å would produce that dipole increment:
$$
\frac{0.78(3.33564\times10^{-30})}{10^{-10}}
\simeq0.16e.
$$
This scale illustrates a plausible interfacial polarization; the work-function measurement alone does not uniquely determine a transferred charge or <adsorption> geometry.

= d
{parent=11}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}

At higher coverage, neighboring outward dipoles generate a local field opposing each other's polarization. This <dipole depolarization> reduces the incremental dipole per added <molecule>, so the <work function> becomes less sensitive to additional <adsorption>.

Crowding can also tilt the <molecules>, alter their coordination or make them occupy less strongly donating sites. Any of these changes can decrease the normal dipole increment. **A reduced slope is consistent with coverage-dependent depolarization and geometry changes; it is not evidence that coverage has stopped increasing.**

= e
{parent=11}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=e}

A plausible explanation is **partial dissociation leaving a residual surface species**, such as an NH$_x$ fragment, adsorbed nitrogen or hydrogen, after molecular ammonia has finished desorbing. A small residual population can maintain a surface <electric dipole moment> and a $0.2$ eV <work function> offset. Heating farther can remove, recombine or rearrange those species.

Test this hypothesis by recording <temperature-programmed desorption> signals for hydrogen and nitrogen as well as ammonia, and by measuring residual nitrogen with <X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy>. A surface-sensitive vibrational spectrum could distinguish NH$_x$ groups from atomic nitrogen. Isotopically labelled ammonia would help assign desorption products. Correlating disappearance of the residual spectral signal with recovery of the clean-surface <work function> would be more diagnostic than the absence of an ammonia signal alone. This is a proposed explanation, not a uniquely established identification.

