= Solution
{parent=ii}

Treat this as standalone <organophosphorus chemistry>. The phosphorus(III) lone pair attacks the terminal nitrogen of the <organic azide>. Cyclization of the initial phosphazide and loss of nitrogen give an <iminophosphorane>:
$$
\mathrm{ArN_3+PPh_2(C_6H_4CONH_2)
\longrightarrow ArN=PPh_2(C_6H_4CONH_2)+N_2}.
$$
The ortho <amide> provides a neighboring acidic NH <proton> and a <carbonyl> oxygen donor. A <proton> relay to the imino nitrogen and intramolecular O$\to$P attack generate a five-membered, O-bound pentacoordinate phosphorus intermediate. Water can then attack phosphorus, with <proton transfer> to nitrogen, followed by P–N cleavage and opening of the neighboring-group contact.

The overall hydrolysis products are
$$
\boxed{\mathrm{ArNH_2+Ph_2P(O)(C_6H_4CONH_2)+N_2}.}
$$
The <carbonyl> group assists the hydrolysis and is regenerated; the product is **an aniline and the ortho-carboxamide <phosphine oxide>**, rather than an amide-linked ligation product. The favorable five-membered intramolecular interaction and <proton> delivery help hydrolyze a normally rather persistent aryl <iminophosphorane>. This explanation concerns only the printed chemical transformation.

= iii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

**TODO: user-deferred biology.** Protein-labelling applications and their associated cycloaddition discussion is deferred under the current nonbiology scope.

= b
{parent=12}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

The <sydnone> is a mesoionic <1,3-dipole>. Its two <cycloaddition> termini add across the alkyne to give a bicyclic adduct. Subsequent retrocycloaddition expels the ring oxygen and <carbonyl> carbon together as carbon dioxide, leaving an aromatic <pyrazole>.

**The product is 1-$R_1$-4,5-di-$R$-pyrazole, plus CO$_2$.** The <sydnone> nitrogen bearing $R_1$ becomes <pyrazole> N1; the adjacent nitrogen becomes N2; the <sydnone> CH becomes C3; and the two alkyne carbons become C4 and C5. Since both alkyne substituents are the same, reversing its orientation gives the same constitutional product.

Formation of the aromatic <pyrazole> and release of CO$_2$ favor the fragmentation. The net transformation can be understood entirely as a <cycloaddition>–<cycloreversion> sequence; no biological application is needed.

= 13
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}

= a
{parent=13}
{scope}

= i
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

One standard description of sodium-rich NaCl is removal of lattice chloride into a NaCl reservoir, leaving a positively charged chloride vacancy and an <electron>:
$$
\boxed{\mathrm{Na(s)+Cl_{Cl}^{\times}\longrightarrow
NaCl(s)+V_{Cl}^{\bullet}+e'.}}
$$
This <Kröger–Vink notation> equation conserves atoms, chloride sites and effective charge. The <electron> can be trapped in the vacancy, forming an electrically neutral <F center>:
$$
\mathrm{V_{Cl}^{\bullet}+e'\rightleftharpoons
(V_{Cl}^{\bullet}e')^{\times}}.
$$
Thus sodium excess introduces electronic defects and can give coloration. **It does not imply that every added <electron> is a mobile conduction <electron>**: electronic transport depends on trapping and thermal release. Chloride vacancies can also assist anion transport. An alternative microscopic incorporation route is a sodium interstitial plus an <electron>, but the vacancy–<electron> model describes the usual alkali-metal-colored halide.

= ii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

The trivalent dopant is comparable in radius to Na$^+$ and is naturally accommodated on a sodium site rather than a chloride site. One charge-balanced incorporation reaction is
$$
\boxed{\mathrm{YCl_3\longrightarrow
Y_{Na}^{\bullet\bullet}+2V_{Na}'+3Cl_{Cl}^{\times}}.}
$$
Three cation sites are filled by one Y ion and two vacancies; the three chloride ions occupy the corresponding anion sites. The effective $+2$ substitutional charge is balanced by two singly negative sodium vacancies.

**YCl$_3$ doping primarily increases sodium-vacancy-mediated ionic conductivity**, provided the vacancies remain mobile rather than strongly bound to the dopant. In contrast, sodium-rich doping introduces <electrons> and anion vacancies or interstitial-related defects. Neither mechanism requires holes as its compensating carrier, and an increase in vacancy number need not translate proportionally into conductivity if defect association reduces mobility.

= b
{parent=13}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

The <diffusion coefficient> relates particle flux to concentration gradient through <Fick's first law>, $\mathbf j=-D\nabla c$. For unbiased isotropic motion in $d$ dimensions it can equivalently be defined by the long-time <mean squared displacement>:
$$
\boxed{D=\lim_{t\to\infty}\frac{\langle|\mathbf r(t)-\mathbf r(0)|^2\rangle}{2dt}.}
$$
An uncorrelated <random walk> with jump length $a$ and total jump rate $\Gamma$ has $\langle r^2\rangle=\Gamma a^2t$, hence $D=\Gamma a^2/(2d)$; correlations introduce a multiplicative correlation factor. The factor $d$ counts the directions sharing the total displacement. It does not mean that the same material's single-coordinate diffusivity changes merely by choosing to observe fewer coordinates: $\langle x^2\rangle=2Dt$ in every Cartesian direction of the same isotropic system.

At $500$ K, silver iodide is in its fast-ion-conducting $\alpha$ phase. For an order-of-magnitude estimate, take a jump length $a\sim3$ Å and an effective successful jump rate $\Gamma\sim10^{11}\ \mathrm{s^{-1}}$. Then
$$
\boxed{D\sim\frac{(3\times10^{-10})^2(10^{11})}{6}
\sim1.5\times10^{-9}\ \mathrm{m^2\,s^{-1}}.}
$$
Thus **$D$ of order $10^{-9}\ \mathrm{m^2\,s^{-1}}$** is a reasonable estimate for this <superionic conductor>. The length and rate are explicitly assumed material scales; the question supplies neither a measured conductivity nor jump data, so an exact numerical coefficient cannot be deduced from <temperature> alone. The estimate is not presented as an experimentally measured value.

= c
{parent=13}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

A one-dimensional <probability density> has units $L^{-1}$, while $D$ has units $L^2T^{-1}$. With no other length scale, <dimensional analysis> requires
$$
\boxed{n(x,t)=\frac1{\sqrt{Dt}}F\left(\frac{x}{\sqrt{Dt}}\right).}
$$
Normalization gives $\int_{-\infty}^{\infty}F(\xi)\,d\xi=1$ and unbiased <diffusion> gives an even $F$. **Dimensional arguments alone do not specify $F$.**

For ordinary Fickian <diffusion>, impose $\partial_t n=D\partial_x^2 n$ with $n(x,0)=\delta(x)$. Substitution of the similarity form yields
$$
F''+\tfrac12(\xi F)'=0.
$$
A decaying symmetric solution obeys $F'=-\xi F/2$, so $F=C e^{-\xi^2/4}$. Normalization fixes $C=1/\sqrt{4\pi}$:
$$
\boxed{n(x,t)=\frac1{\sqrt{4\pi Dt}}
\exp\left(-\frac{x^2}{4Dt}\right),\qquad t>0.}
$$
This <heat kernel> integrates to one, has <variance> $2Dt$, and tends to the initial <Dirac delta distribution> as $t\downarrow0$.

= 14
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}

= a
{parent=14}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

**Product 5 is 1-methylcyclohexan-1-ol.** The tertiary ring C–H bond of methylcyclohexane is replaced by C–OH. This is a single constitutional product; the two ring paths at that carbon are equivalent, so no new <stereocenter> is created there.

The manganese–oxo species abstracts the relatively weak tertiary hydrogen, then hydroxyl rebound converts the carbon radical into the tertiary <alcohol>. The unfunctionalized <catalyst> has no crown-ether receptor to preassociate the ammonium substrate. Its selectivity therefore mainly follows intrinsic C–H reactivity: the tertiary site of methylcyclohexane outcompetes the secondary C–H sites of the linear chain. The cationic ammonium group also electronically deactivates nearby sites. This explains the dominant product under the idealized selectivity described in the question.

= b
{parent=14}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

**Product 6 is a remotely oxidized <ketone> of the undecylammonium chain**, retaining the ammonium group and its counterion. Number from the ammonium-bearing carbon as C1. Crown-mediated recognition positions the remote C8–C9 region near the Mn–oxo center. A representative major-site product is the C8 <ketone>,
$$
\boxed{\mathrm{[H_3N^+-(CH_2)_7-C(O)-(CH_2)_2-CH_3]BF_4^-}.}
$$
Its adjacent possible regioisomer is the C9 <ketone> $\mathrm{[H_3N^+-(CH_2)_8-C(O)-CH_2-CH_3]BF_4^-}$. The precise single-site assignment is a limitation discussed below.

The <crown ether> binds the primary ammonium end through several N–H···O <hydrogen bonds>. This <host–guest complex> holds a folded portion of the chain close to the manganese oxidant, so the effective local concentration of a remote secondary C–H bond can overcome the intrinsic advantage of a free tertiary C–H bond. Such recognition-driven reversal of substrate preference is experimentally established.<Enzyme-like substrate-selectivity in C–H oxidation enabled by recognition by Giorgio Olivo and others>[Abstract and competitive substrate oxidation experiments]{ref}

The key intermediates are the crown-bound ammonium–Mn=O assembly, a remote carbon radical after H abstraction, and the rebound secondary <alcohol>. A second oxidation removes the remaining hydrogen on the carbinol carbon to give the <ketone>. The overall oxygen-equivalent count is
$$
\mathrm{RCH_2R'+H_2O_2\to RCH(OH)R'+H_2O},
\qquad
\mathrm{RCH(OH)R'+H_2O_2\to RC(O)R'+2H_2O}.
$$
Thus **one <ketone> requires two equivalents of peroxide**, explaining a maximum near 50% <ketone> yield with one equivalent and near-complete conversion with two.

The primary remote-oxidation study reports preference for **C8 and C9**, not a universally unique carbon. The schematic <catalyst> drawing and the oxygen budget alone cannot distinguish those positions quantitatively. The “single product” wording is therefore treated as an idealized remote-site assignment; a uniquely verified C8:C9 ratio is not invented.<Supramolecular Recognition Allows Remote, Site-Selective C–H Oxidation of Methylenic Sites in Linear Amines by Giorgio Olivo and others>[Abstract, remote C8–C9 selectivity]{ref}

= c
{parent=14}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

Ba$^{2+}$ competes strongly for the <crown ether> receptors. With the recognition sites occupied, the undecylammonium chain can no longer be effectively preorganized near manganese. **The reaction should move back towards oxidation of methylcyclohexane to 1-methylcyclohexan-1-ol**, with suppression of the selectively formed remote amino <ketone>.

The single available peroxide equivalent is then sufficient for tertiary C–H hydroxylation, whereas a secondary-chain <ketone> would require two oxidations. Competitive binding constants and metal-induced structural changes would determine the exact conversion and residual selectivity; neither a numerical yield nor complete inhibition of all oxidation follows merely from adding two equivalents of barium salt.

= d
{parent=14}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}

Without a competing hydrocarbon and without a crown receptor, <catalyst> 1 can still oxidize accessible secondary C–H bonds in the chain. **Expect a less site-selective mixture of secondary oxidation products, predominantly <ketones> when two peroxide equivalents are effectively used**, rather than the recognition-selected remote product pattern of <catalyst> 2.

The strongly electron-withdrawing, protonated ammonium end makes nearby C–H bonds less reactive, so the distribution need not be statistically uniform over every methylene. More distant secondary positions are comparatively favorable, while primary terminal C–H oxidation is generally harder. <alcohol> intermediates, incomplete conversion and unproductive peroxide consumption are also possible. Exact regioisomer ratios or a guaranteed quantitative yield cannot be predicted from the supplied conditions.

= 15
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}

= a
{parent=15}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

A <SMILES string> encodes a molecular <graph> as a sequence of atom and bond symbols, with parentheses for branches, matching digits for ring closures and additional symbols for charges and <stereochemistry>. For example, ``CCO`` specifies ethanol. A <molecule> may have several valid SMILES traversals, so the raw character sequence is not itself a unique physical descriptor.

Two numerical encodings are:

* A <molecular fingerprint>: a fixed-length vector indicating local substructures, for example hashed circular neighborhoods around atoms. Each coordinate is a bit or a count. The same atom–bond graph should produce the same fingerprint regardless of its SMILES traversal.
* A <molecular descriptor> vector: real-valued quantities such as <molar mass>, atom counts, formal charge, <hydrogen bond donor> and <hydrogen bond acceptor> counts, or topological indices. Standardize these coordinates using the training set before fitting a scale-sensitive model.

These are chemical-structure representations. No biological mechanism is needed to encode the <molecules> or treat the supplied target as a binary label.

= b
{parent=15}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Include an intercept $w_0$ and let $\mathbf z$ be the chosen numerical feature vector. <Logistic regression> uses
$$
\boxed{h(\mathbf z;\mathbf w)=\sigma(w_0+\mathbf w^{\mathsf T}\mathbf z),
\qquad\sigma(u)=\frac1{1+e^{-u}}.}
$$
The <logistic function> maps every real score into $(0,1)$, allowing a <Bernoulli distribution> model for the label. Equivalently, the <log odds> are linear in the features:
$$
\log\frac h{1-h}=w_0+\mathbf w^{\mathsf T}\mathbf z.
$$
This gives a smooth differentiable <probability> model with a linear decision boundary at any fixed <probability> threshold. The sigmoid output is a fitted conditional <probability>, not a categorical prediction until a threshold is chosen.

= c
{parent=15}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

For conditionally independent binary labels, the <likelihood> is
$$
L(\mathbf w)=\prod_{i=1}^{m} h_i^{y_i}(1-h_i)^{1-y_i},\qquad
h_i=h(\mathbf z_i;\mathbf w).
$$
Maximizing it is equivalent to minimizing its negative <log likelihood>, the mean <binary cross-entropy>:
$$
\boxed{\mathcal L(\mathbf w)=-\frac1m\sum_{i=1}^{m}
[y_i\log h_i+(1-y_i)\log(1-h_i)].}
$$
It is the loss implied by a <Bernoulli distribution> for each target, penalizes confident errors strongly and is convex in the linear-logistic parameters. For numerical stability, each contribution can be evaluated as $\log(1+e^{u_i})-y_i u_i$ using a stable softplus implementation.

The 10:90 class imbalance does not invalidate this <likelihood>. Class weighting or resampling may be useful if errors have unequal costs, but changes the training objective and potentially the interpretation of fitted <probabilities>. Optional <ridge regression> adds $\lambda\|\mathbf w\|^2/2$, usually excluding the intercept, to control overly large weights or complete separation.

= d
{parent=15}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}

Initialize the weights, evaluate the feature matrix and repeat <gradient descent> updates until the loss or gradient stabilizes. If $\widetilde{\mathbf z}_i=(1,\mathbf z_i)$ includes the intercept, then
$$
\nabla\mathcal L=\frac1m\sum_i(h_i-y_i)\widetilde{\mathbf z}_i,
\qquad
\mathbf w\leftarrow\mathbf w-\eta\nabla\mathcal L.
$$
A positive step size $\eta$ chosen by a convergence rule or <line search> gives a practical optimization algorithm. Mini-batch updates or a Newton-type method are alternatives. Fit feature scaling, choose regularization and choose any nonstandard threshold using training or validation data, then freeze the procedure before using the held-out test set.

For $M$ held-out examples, set $\widehat Y_i=\mathbf1\{h(\mathbf Z_i;\widehat{\mathbf w})\geq t\}$, with $t=0.5$ unless selected otherwise. The requested <classification accuracy> is
$$
\boxed{\mathrm{accuracy}=\frac1M\sum_{i=1}^M\mathbf1\{\widehat Y_i=Y_i\}
=\frac{TP+TN}{TP+TN+FP+FN}.}
$$
**Always predicting the majority class already gives about 90% accuracy.** Report a <confusion matrix>, <classification recall> and <classification precision> as well, especially for the minority class. A <precision-recall curve> shows the threshold tradeoff without confusing high majority-class accuracy with successful minority-class detection. No numerical test accuracy can be calculated without the held-out predictions and labels.

= 16
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}

= a
{parent=16}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

The first step is an endo-selective <Diels–Alder reaction> of the pyrone diene with the electron-poor acrylamide. Number the pyrone $O1-C2(=O)-C3(OH)=C4-C5=C6$, and the acrylamide carbons $C_\alpha$ and $C_\beta$, with $C_\alpha$ next to its <carbonyl>. The favored polar connection is **C3–C$_\alpha$ and C6–C$_\beta$**, leaving C4=C5 and the original C2–O1 lactone bridge intact.

Lithium allyloxide, generated from <n-butyllithium> and allyl <alcohol>, attacks the bridging lactone <carbonyl>. Collapse of the <tetrahedral intermediate> cleaves its acyl–oxygen bond. <proton transfer> converts the former O1 bridge into an OH substituent at C6 and leaves the former pyrone <carbonyl> as an allyl <ester> attached to C3. The morpholine <amide> is retained.

Thus **C is allyl 1,4-dihydroxy-2-(morpholine-4-carbonyl)cyclohex-5-ene-1-carboxylate**, with the <stereochemistry> described below. In this product numbering C1 is the former pyrone C3, C2 is the acrylamide $C_\alpha$, C3 is $C_\beta$, and C4 is the former pyrone C6.

\Image[paper-1-pyrone-product.png]
{title=Predicted ring-opened pyrone cycloadduct with explicit relative stereochemistry}

The bridge fixes the former C3 carboxyl group and C6 oxygen on the same face. An endo acrylamide places its <amide> substituent on that face too. Accordingly **CO$_2$allyl at C1, the C2 <amide> and the C4 OH are cis; the C1 OH is on the opposite face**. The alternative face of attack gives the <enantiomer>, so achiral reagents give a racemate. The $1731\ \mathrm{cm^{-1}}$ band is consistent with the newly formed <ester>, while the strong lower-frequency band is consistent with the retained, strongly polarized <amide> <carbonyl>. These bands support carbonyl-class retention; they do not independently prove the predicted regioisomer or <stereochemistry>.

= b
{parent=16}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

The terminal alkene of D first undergoes <Wacker oxidation>, giving the side chain $\mathrm{Ar-CH_2-CH_2-COCH_3}$. Alkene coordination to Pd(II), water attack, <proton transfers> and <beta-hydride elimination> give the <ketone>. Copper and oxygen regenerate the active palladium oxidation state.

Base removes a <proton> from the terminal methyl of that <ketone>. Its <enolate> attacks the neighboring aromatic aldehyde intramolecularly, closing a six-membered ring. Dehydration gives a conjugated cyclohexadienone-type intermediate, and <keto-enol tautomerism> furnishes aromatic naphthol E. The retained methoxy and MOM-protected oxygen groups follow the original aromatic-carbon positions.

**F is the O-allyl ether of E.** Carbonate forms the naphthoxide and allyl bromide undergoes an <SN2 reaction> at its terminal carbon. Heating F then causes a \[3,3\]-<Claisen rearrangement>: the O–allyl bond breaks, the terminal allyl carbon bonds to the adjacent naphthalene C1, and the phenolic carbon temporarily becomes a <ketone>. Rearomatization gives G, with a free phenolic OH and a C1 allyl group. The C1 position gives the favored alpha-substituted naphthol and avoids the alternative less favorable annulation pattern.

The final step is a **dearomative <Michael addition>**, not a Nef conversion of the nitro group. Hydrogen bonding from the thiourea activates nitroethylene. The nucleophilic C1 resonance form of G attacks the terminal carbon of $\mathrm{CH_2=CHNO_2}$, and <proton transfer> converts the nitronate to a 2-nitroethyl substituent. The phenolic carbon becomes a <ketone>.

**H is the corresponding 1-allyl-1-(2-nitroethyl)naphthalen-2-one**, retaining the methoxy and MOM substituents of G. The second ring now has a C2=O group and a C3=C4 bond, while the other ring remains aromatic. The reported $^{13}$C signal near $202$ ppm is consistent with that newly formed <ketone>. Related dearomative additions establish this reaction class.<Asymmetric Dearomatization of β-Naphthols through a Bifunctional-Thiourea-Catalyzed Michael Reaction by Shou-Guo Wang and others>[Abstract, addition of nitroethylene to alpha-substituted beta-naphthols]{ref}

C1 is now an all-carbon quaternary <stereocenter> bearing both allyl and 2-nitroethyl groups. Both the drawn thiourea and the starting materials are achiral, so **H is racemic**. A chiral <catalyst> would be needed to select one absolute configuration.

\Image[paper-1-naphthol-products.png]
{title=O-allyl intermediate and dearomative Michael-addition product}

